+  (baseline-r0/0/0 sha 530c single vs 4694 dual), so cross-phase comparison is
+  meaningless; spec also changes the decoding path (checkpointed ngram drafts), so
+  per-phase identity is the only valid comparison.
+- VRAM identical to off (9507 MiB used / 10240 total) in all 4 phases.
+
+### B choice: `ngram-cache` (a second ngram type)
+
+B = `--spec-type ngram-cache`. Justification:
+- A (ngram-mod) already answers the agent question positively in single-agent but
+  not in dual. The open question is whether **any** ngram type helps when both slots
+  are busy, which is the deployment rule.
+- `ngram-cache` is the most distinct mechanism: it **persists the n-gram pool across
+  requests** on the server (vs per-context in ngram-mod), which is exactly the
+  "agent re-emits file contents, diffs, test output" and "both slots constantly
+  generating" traffic. If per-context ngram-mod's low dual acceptance (9-14%) comes
+  from an under-populated per-request pool, a cross-request cache is the setting
+  most likely to move the dual-phase number.
+- It is listed in spec_flags.txt and is a clean single-flag swap, keeping all other
+  production flags and the per-request max_tokens identical to A.
